# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 gen.cc pow and sym2poly.cc normal. (1) pow: (e^a)^b was rewritten as e^(a*b) for any a and any non-integer b, a rule that holds only when im(a) lies in (-pi,pi]: sqrt(exp(4*i)) gave exp(2*i), about -0.416+0.909*i (the principal root is -exp(2*i), 0.416-0.909*i), sqrt(exp(2+4*i)) the negation too, and sqrt(exp(3*acosh(-2))) gave -i*(2+sqrt(3))^(3/2) (right +i*(2+sqrt(3))^(3/2): exp(3*acosh(-2)) is a negative real). Now the principal value e^(b*(a-2*k*pi*i)) is taken, k the integer that brings im(a)-2*k*pi into (-pi,pi], decided on the double of im(a) with a margin of 1e-9 at the half-integers of im(a)/(2*pi); at the cut (2*m+1)*pi a k is taken only where recursive_normal proves im(a)-(2*m+1)*pi = 0 (then arg = +pi), else the power stays as typed and its evalf is the principal value; so does an im(a) past 2^21*pi. An integer b, im(a) inside (-pi,pi) and an a with no numeric value (a free x) keep the rule. (2) normal and simplify: in_normalize_sqrt took sqrt(s*d^2/den^2) as abs(d)/abs(den)*sqrt(s) and sym2rxroot the d-th root of D^d*S as D times that of S for any d, D with no literal i; acosh(-1/2) is ln(-1/2+sqrt(-1/2+1)*sqrt(-1/2-1)), whose value 2*pi*i/3 has no literal i, so normal(sqrt(acosh(-1/2)^3)) gave abs(z)*sqrt(z) (2.143+2.143*i) and simplify z*sqrt(z) (-2.143+2.143*i), right 2.143-2.143*i. Now a factor whose double is complex (a numeric value that is not real) is not taken out of the root.
